Caption: Fig. 3.68. CMSX-4 creep testpiece interrupted after 2.2% strain after 200 h at 1150 °C and 100 MPa (see the creep curve marked ‘F’ in Figure 3.67). (a) Necked creep specimen at point of fracture. (b) Micrograph of creep-cavitated casting porosity in necked region. (c) Variation of fraction of pores cavitated as a function of distance along specimen, providing confirmation that cavitation is restricted to the necked region.
Description: This composite figure shows results from an interrupted creep test on a CMSX-4 specimen. (a) is a photograph of the testpiece, showing significant necking at the fracture point. (b) is a micrograph revealing creep-cavitated porosity within this necked region, with a 100 µm scale bar. (c) is a chart plotting the fraction of cavitated pores versus position along the gauge length, showing a distinct peak that confirms cavitation is localized to the necked area. The caption links this data to point 'F' on the creep curve in Figure 3.67.
