Caption: Fig. 4.22. Optical micrographs of RR1000 superalloy: (a) sub-solvus heat-treated for 4 h at 1130°C; (b) super-solvus heat-treated for 4 h at 1170 °C. The solvus temperature in this alloy is about 1150 °C. Note the grain-pinning effect, which occurs when the heat-treatment temperature is sub-solvus. (Courtesy of Rob Mitchell.)
Description: Two optical micrographs of RR1000 superalloy showing the effect of heat treatment temperature. (a) Sub-solvus treatment at 1130°C results in a fine-grained structure with primary gamma-prime particles pinning the grain boundaries (scale bar 20µm). (b) Super-solvus treatment at 1170°C results in a much coarser grain structure as the pinning particles have dissolved (scale bar 200µm).
